Scheduling and Managing Cron Jobs
===============================================================
Goal: Check existing cron jobs, create an every-minute job, check
the log, add a 7:00 AM daily job and a Sunday 11:59 PM job, list
all jobs, then remove the every-minute job and verify.

CRON FORMAT (5 time fields + command):
  minute  hour  day-of-month  month  day-of-week  command
  0-59    0-23  1-31          1-12   0-7 (0 or 7 = Sunday)

Replace "centos" with your own username everywhere below.


STEP 0: Make the folder and the script
--------------------------------------
$ mkdir -p ~/cron
$ nano ~/cron/create.sh

Type this inside the script:

  #!/bin/bash
  PATH_DIR="/home/centos/cron"
  /usr/bin/touch $PATH_DIR/morning_task
  echo "This is crontab" >> $PATH_DIR/morning_task

Save in nano: Ctrl+O, Enter, then Ctrl+X

Make it executable (cron needs this):
$ chmod +x ~/cron/create.sh

Test it once by hand:
$ ~/cron/create.sh
$ cat ~/cron/morning_task

Make sure the cron service is running:
$ sudo systemctl status crond
(if not running: sudo systemctl enable --now crond)


STEP 1: Check whether the current user has any cron jobs
--------------------------------------------------------
$ crontab -l

If none exist, it prints: no crontab for centos
(In your screenshot the output was empty, which also means none.)


STEP 2: Create a cron job that runs every minute
------------------------------------------------
$ crontab -e

Press i (insert mode) in vim, then type:

* * * * * /home/centos/cron/create.sh

Save and exit vim: press Esc, then type  :wq  and press Enter
(You should see: crontab: installing new crontab)

Check it:
$ crontab -l


STEP 3: Wait a few minutes and verify the log is updated
--------------------------------------------------------
Wait 2-3 minutes, then:

$ sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/cron

Or watch it live (Ctrl+C to stop):
$ sudo tail -f /var/log/cron

You should see lines like:
  CROND[...]: (centos) CMD (/home/centos/cron/create.sh)
  CROND[...]: (centos) CMDEND (/home/centos/cron/create.sh)

Alternative check, see the file the job writes to:
$ cat ~/cron/morning_task


STEP 4: Schedule a job at 7:00 AM daily that creates morning_task.txt
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Create the target folder first:
$ mkdir -p ~/cron_tasks

$ crontab -e

Add this line:

0 7 * * * /usr/bin/touch /home/centos/cron_tasks/morning_task.txt

(The task asks for a file named morning_task.txt in
 /home/<username>/cron_tasks. Your screenshot used create.sh and
 created "morning_task" in the cron folder, which is slightly
 different. The line above matches the task exactly. If your
 teacher accepts create.sh, use:
 0 7 * * * /home/centos/cron/create.sh)


STEP 5: Schedule a job to run every Sunday at 11:59 PM
------------------------------------------------------
$ crontab -e

Add this line:

59 23 * * 0 /home/centos/cron/create.sh

(The last field 0 = Sunday. Your screenshot shows "59 23 * * *",
 which runs EVERY day. Use "59 23 * * 0" for Sunday only.)

Save and exit: Esc, then :wq


STEP 6: List the cron jobs and verify all are present
-----------------------------------------------------
$ crontab -l

Expected (3 jobs):
* * * * * /home/centos/cron/create.sh
0 7 * * * /usr/bin/touch /home/centos/cron_tasks/morning_task.txt
59 23 * * 0 /home/centos/cron/create.sh


STEP 7: Remove the every-minute job
-----------------------------------
$ crontab -e

Delete the line that starts with  * * * * *
In vim: move the cursor to that line and press  dd
Save and exit: Esc, then :wq


STEP 8: Verify it was deleted
-----------------------------
$ crontab -l

The "* * * * *" line should be gone. Only the 7:00 AM job and the
Sunday job remain.

Extra check (no new entries should appear after a minute or two):
$ sudo tail -n 10 /var/log/cron


EXTRA COMMANDS (not needed, but useful)
---------------------------------------
crontab -r     -> remove ALL your cron jobs (careful!)
crontab -l -u <user>   -> list another user's jobs (needs sudo)


QUICK COMMAND SUMMARY
---------------------
crontab -l                          -> list jobs
crontab -e                          -> edit jobs
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/cron       -> check cron log
* * * * *    <command>              -> every minute
0 7 * * *    <command>              -> 7:00 AM daily
59 23 * * 0  <command>              -> Sunday 11:59 PM
dd (in vim) then :wq                -> delete a line and save
